--TEST--
`Core\Secret::reveal` and `::revealBytes` answer their operand with the `secret` qualifier dropped, and every other `Core` member still refuses one — `rule:core-classes/secret-reveal`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/secret-reveal`'s one narrow removal outside a checked conversion: forbidden by
// default, rare, greppable, and carrying a written reason at the call site.
// Every refusal § 4 states — `echo`, `Core\Json::encode`, the copy boundary —
// names this call in its help text, so until it exists that help is a dead end.
secret string $token = "hunter2";
secret bytes $key = "raw-key" as bytes;

// The declared type is the assertion. A `string` binding accepts nothing that
// still carries the qualifier, so a `reveal` that answered `secret string` —
// which is what a type variable would have bound to, and the one failure this
// surface cannot have — stops the case here rather than at the sink below.
string $revealed = Core\Secret::reveal($token, "this command's whole purpose is printing the token");
bytes $raw = Core\Secret::revealBytes($key, "the signer takes the key as bytes");

// The second half of the same claim, read at `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse`'s terminal sink:
// `echo` refuses a `secret` operand outright, so reaching output at all says
// the qualifier is gone rather than merely re-declared away.
echo $revealed, "\n";
echo Core\Bytes::length($raw), "\n";

// The reason is checked as an ordinary `string` and read by nobody — § 3 asks
// for it to be written, not for it to be a literal, so a binding holding one
// is as greppable at the call site as the text would have been.
string $why = "the audit log records which command disclosed it";
echo Core\Secret::reveal($token, $why), "\n";

// Revealing a value that is not `secret` is the identity. There is no refusal
// for it: it costs a diagnostic and prevents no exposure, which is
// `nvs_stdlib::registry`'s own reason for `Qual::Reveal` being a mark rather
// than a `CoreTy`.
string $plain = "plain";
echo Core\Secret::reveal($plain, "nothing to reveal, and that is legal"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
hunter2
7
hunter2
plain
